<main>
<div class=3D"part" id=3D"part1"><div class=3D"part-kick">Part 1 =C2=B7 Ste=
ps 1=E2=80=938</div><h2>Plan and write</h2><p class=3D"part-sub">Set up the=
 practice server and the studio, give it a brand voice, plan a campaign and=
 write the messages. Nothing gets sent in this part.</p></div>
<section class=3D"step" id=3D"step-1" data-step=3D"1">
  <div class=3D"step-head"><div class=3D"num">1</div><div class=3D"meta"><d=
iv class=3D"cap"><span class=3D"cap-text">Skill: looking for trusted skills=
 and servers</span><span class=3D"cap-text">Real-world habit</span><span cl=
ass=3D"xchip">2.4</span></div><h3>Practice server, then look before you bui=
ld</h3><p class=3D"sub">Set up the Twilio practice server on Windows 11, th=
en search for skills and MCP servers other people made, and decide which on=
es to trust.</p></div><label class=3D"done-box"><input type=3D"checkbox" id=
=3D"done-1"> Done</label></div>
  <div class=3D"plain"><p><strong>In plain words:</strong> the practice ser=
ver is a pretend Twilio that lives on your own laptop. Messages go in, get =
a real-looking receipt, and never reach a phone. A skill is like an app for=
 Claude. Before you build one, look in the app store. Download counts can b=
e faked, so also check who made it.</p></div>

  <div class=3D"part-a" id=3D"a-setup">
    <h4 style=3D"margin-top:0">Part A =C2=B7 Set up the Twilio practice ser=
ver on Windows 11</h4>
    <p>You do this once. It uses the same tool your Email Studio used for R=
esend, <code>emulate</code>, version 0.12.3, which also emulates Twilio. No=
thing needs a Twilio account.</p>

    <p><strong>A1. Check your tools.</strong> Open PowerShell and run:</p>
    <div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-head"><span class=3D"chip run">R=
UN</span><span class=3D"code-desc">PowerShell</span></div><pre>node --versi=
on
npx --version</pre></div>
    <p>Node must say v22 or higher. If <code>npx</code> says <em>"running s=
cripts is disabled on this system"</em>, allow scripts for your own user, a=
nswer <code>Y</code>, then open a new PowerShell window:</p>
    <div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-head"><span class=3D"chip run">R=
UN</span><span class=3D"code-desc">PowerShell</span></div><pre>Set-Executio=
nPolicy -Scope CurrentUser -ExecutionPolicy RemoteSigned</pre></div>

    <p><strong>A2. Start the practice server in a second window.</strong> O=
pen a <strong>new</strong> PowerShell tab (this becomes your server window)=
 and run:</p>
    <div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-head"><span class=3D"chip run">R=
UN</span><span class=3D"code-desc">PowerShell</span></div><pre>npx emulate@=
0.12.3 start --service twilio</pre></div>
    <p>The first time, npx asks <em>"Need to install the following packages=
: emulate@0.12.3. Ok to proceed? (y)"</em>. Type <code>y</code>. Wait until=
 you see:</p>
    <div class=3D"output">emulate

  twilio  http://localhost:4000
    Source: twilio

  Ready.</div>
    <p>If Windows Defender Firewall asks about Node.js, choose <strong>Priv=
ate networks</strong> only, or Cancel. The server only listens on your own =
laptop, so it works either way. Leave this window open whenever you use the=
 studio. <code>Ctrl+C</code> stops it.</p>

    <p><strong>A3. Look at it in your browser.</strong> Open <code>http://1=
27.0.0.1:4000</code>. You should see a page titled <strong>Twilio Inspector=
</strong>. This is where your practice messages will appear.</p>

    <p><strong>A4. Test it with real API calls.</strong> Go back to your <s=
trong>first</strong> PowerShell window. Paste these three lines to save the=
 practice login. They are the server's built-in practice values, not real s=
ecrets:</p>
    <div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-head"><span class=3D"chip run">R=
UN</span><span class=3D"code-desc">PowerShell</span></div><pre>$sid  =3D "A=
C00000000000000000000000000000000"
$auth =3D "${sid}:twilio_test_auth_token"
$api  =3D "http://127.0.0.1:4000/2010-04-01/Accounts/$sid/Messages.json"</p=
re></div>
    <p>Always type <code>curl.exe</code>, with <code>.exe</code>. In Window=
s PowerShell, plain <code>curl</code> is a different command and gives conf=
using errors.</p>

    <p><em>Test 1: list messages.</em> It should be empty:</p>
    <div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-head"><span class=3D"chip run">R=
UN</span><span class=3D"code-desc">PowerShell</span></div><pre>curl.exe -s =
-u $auth $api</pre></div>
    <div class=3D"output">{"messages":[],"end":0, ... }</div>

    <p><em>Test 2: send a practice text</em> to Twilio's test number <code>=
+15005550006</code>, from the server's built-in sender number <code>+155512=
34567</code>:</p>
    <div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-head"><span class=3D"chip run">R=
UN</span><span class=3D"code-desc">PowerShell</span></div><pre>curl.exe -s =
-u $auth -X POST $api --data-urlencode "To=3D+15005550006" --data-urlencode=
 "From=3D+15551234567" --data-urlencode "Body=3DHello from my SMS studio"</=
pre></div>
    <p>You get back JSON with a message id starting <code>"sid":"SM</code>,=
 plus <code>"status":"queued"</code> and <code>"num_segments":"1"</code>.</=
p>

    <p><em>Test 3: see it arrive.</em> Run Test 1 again. The message is now=
 in the list. Refresh the Twilio Inspector page: it shows there too.</p>

    <p><em>Test 4: a wrong password is refused.</em> This should print <cod=
e>401</code>:</p>
    <div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-head"><span class=3D"chip run">R=
UN</span><span class=3D"code-desc">PowerShell</span></div><pre>curl.exe -s =
-o NUL -w "%{http_code}" -u "${sid}:wrong" $api</pre></div>

    <p><em>Test 5: long messages split.</em> Send a 170-character message. =
It should report <code>"num_segments":"2"</code>, which means it would be c=
harged as two texts. Remember this for Step 6.</p>
    <div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-head"><span class=3D"chip run">R=
UN</span><span class=3D"code-desc">PowerShell</span></div><pre>curl.exe -s =
-u $auth -X POST $api --data-urlencode "To=3D+15005550006" --data-urlencode=
 "From=3D+15551234567" --data-urlencode "Body=3D$('a' * 170)"</pre></div>

    <p><strong>Good to know:</strong></p>
    <ul class=3D"plainlist">
      <li>Use <code>127.0.0.1</code>, not <code>localhost</code>, in comman=
ds and settings. The server only answers on 127.0.0.1, and on Windows "loca=
lhost" sometimes tries the IPv6 address first and fails to connect. Your br=
owser copes with either.</li>
      <li>Sent messages stay <code>queued</code>. A real phone network woul=
d mark them delivered. In Step 9 your connection marks them delivered for y=
ou.</li>
      <li>Restarting the server wipes the message list. That's fine: every =
practice run starts clean.</li>
    </ul>

    <p><strong>If something goes wrong:</strong></p>
    <div class=3D"table-wrap"><table>
      <tr><th>You see</th><th>Fix</th></tr>
      <tr><td><code>npx</code> or <code>node</code> is not recognized</td><=
td>Close and reopen PowerShell after installing Node. If it still fails, re=
install Node with "Add to PATH" ticked.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>running scripts is disabled</td><td>Run the <code>Set-Executi=
onPolicy</code> line in A1, then open a new window.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>EADDRINUSE</code> or port 4000 is in use</td><td>Start =
on another port: <code>npx emulate@0.12.3 start --service twilio --port 410=
0</code>. Use 4100 in every address, and in Step 9 set <code>TWILIO_BASE_UR=
L</code> to <code>http://127.0.0.1:4100</code>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>curl: Failed to connect</td><td>The server window was closed =
or stopped. Start it again (A2).</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A parameter cannot be found that matches parameter name 'u'</=
td><td>You typed <code>curl</code>. Type <code>curl.exe</code>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>"code":21606</code>, From number not owned</td><td>The =
From number must be <code>+15551234567</code>.</td></tr>
    </table></div>
  </div>

  <h4>Part B =C2=B7 Look before you build</h4>
  <div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-head"><span class=3D"chip build">B=
UILD</span><span class=3D"code-desc">Paste in Claude Code</span></div><pre>=
I'm setting up an SMS marketing studio in this folder. Before I build anyth=
ing myself, I want to find skills and MCP servers other people have already=
 made.
1. Confirm the Twilio practice server is running: use curl to list messages=
 at http://127.0.0.1:4000/2010-04-01/Accounts/AC000000000000000000000000000=
00000/Messages.json with the login AC00000000000000000000000000000000:twili=
o_test_auth_token. Tell me how many messages are there.
2. Search the skills directory at skills.sh by running these one at a time:
npx skills find sms marketing
npx skills find twilio
npx skills find campaign plan
npx skills find text message copy
3. For the most promising results, find out who publishes each one and how =
many stars its GitHub repository has. Look out for official publishers such=
 as anthropics, twilio-labs and vercel-labs.
4. Look up Twilio's official MCP server (@twilio-alpha/mcp, from twilio-lab=
s). Find out whether it can be pointed at a local practice server at http:/=
/127.0.0.1:4000 instead of the real Twilio.
5. Write notes/skills-i-chose.md in plain English with four short sections:
- Use these: the two sources you recommend for writing campaigns and sendin=
g text messages, and why.
- Avoid these: two results you would not trust, and why.
- The MCP decision: should we use Twilio's official MCP server, or build a =
small one of our own for practice mode? Why?
- How I decided: the three things I should check before installing any skil=
l or server.
Don't install anything yet.</pre></div>

  <div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-head"><span class=3D"chip check">C=
HECK</span><span class=3D"code-desc">New session, then paste</span></div><p=
re>Check my work in notes/skills-i-chose.md. Don't change any files. Test i=
t, don't just read it:
1. Is the Twilio practice server running? Use curl to send one message from=
 +15551234567 to +15005550006 at http://127.0.0.1:4000/2010-04-01/Accounts/=
AC00000000000000000000000000000000/Messages.json (login AC00000000000000000=
000000000000000:twilio_test_auth_token), then list messages and confirm it =
is there. Give me the message sid.
2. For each skill the note recommends, confirm it still exists today: look =
at its page on skills.sh or its folder in the GitHub repository. Tell me if=
 any has been renamed, merged or removed.
3. Run one of the searches again (for example npx skills find twilio) and c=
onfirm the install numbers in the note are roughly right.
4. Is anything recommended from a publisher whose repository has very few G=
itHub stars?
5. Does the MCP decision match what the official Twilio server's README act=
ually says?
Give me a short table: Check | OK or Problem | What you found.
Then ask me two quick questions, one at a time, to see if I understand why =
install counts alone aren't enough.</pre></div>
  <div class=3D"outcomes"><div class=3D"ocard win"><div class=3D"okick">You=
'll know it worked when</div><ul class=3D"win-list">
    <li>The practice server answers, and the check gets a message sid start=
ing <code>SM</code>.</li>
    <li>The note picks official or well-known sources, like Anthropic's mar=
keting plugin and the vercel-labs <code>emulate</code> Twilio skill.</li>
    <li>It warns you off a result with many installs but only a handful of =
GitHub stars.</li>
    <li>The MCP decision says Twilio's official server documents no setting=
 for a custom address, so it can't easily reach the practice server. We bui=
ld a small one of our own in Step 9. If your note says something else, ask =
Claude to show where it found that.</li>
  </ul></div><div class=3D"ocard exam"><div class=3D"okick">Exam note</div>=
<p>For a standard need, use an existing community MCP server first, and bui=
ld your own only for work that is special to your team. Here the special ne=
ed is practice mode, so a small server of our own is the right call. That i=
s exactly the judgement the exam tests.</p></div></div>
</section>
<section class=3D"step" id=3D"step-2" data-step=3D"2">
  <div class=3D"step-head"><div class=3D"num">2</div><div class=3D"meta"><d=
iv class=3D"cap"><span class=3D"cap-text">Skill areas: plugins, CLAUDE.md</=
span><span class=3D"xchip">3.1</span></div><h3>Set up your studio</h3><p cl=
ass=3D"sub">Install a plugin for this project only, and write the studio's =
CLAUDE.md.</p></div><label class=3D"done-box"><input type=3D"checkbox" id=
=3D"done-2"> Done</label></div>
  <div class=3D"plain"><p><strong>In plain words:</strong> CLAUDE.md is the=
 studio's notice board. Claude reads it every time it starts work here. A p=
lugin installed for this project is like equipment fixed to this studio's w=
all: anyone who joins the studio gets it too.</p></div>
  <div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-head"><span class=3D"chip build">B=
UILD</span><span class=3D"code-desc">Paste in Claude Code</span></div><pre>=
Let's set up my SMS studio in this folder.
1. Install Anthropic's official marketing plugin for this project only, by =
running these two commands:
claude plugin marketplace add anthropics/knowledge-work-plugins --scope pro=
ject
claude plugin install marketing@knowledge-work-plugins --scope project
2. Create four folders: brand, subscribers, campaigns and notes.
3. Write a CLAUDE.md for the studio in plain English. Include: what the stu=
dio is for (planning and writing SMS campaigns for a small mobile network),=
 a one-line description of each folder, and these rules: write in plain Eng=
lish, never send a text message to real people, and ask me before deleting =
anything.
4. Create a .gitignore that ignores .claude/settings.local.json, node_modul=
es and system files like Thumbs.db and .DS_Store.
5. Commit everything with a clear message.
Then tell me what I have to do so the plugin loads.</pre></div>
  <p><strong>Then:</strong> Type <code>/exit</code> and run <code>claude</c=
ode> again. Plugins load when a session starts. Accept the prompts to trust=
 the folder and the plugin. Type <code>/marketing:</code> and you should se=
e the plugin's commands appear.</p>
  <div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-head"><span class=3D"chip check">C=
HECK</span><span class=3D"code-desc">New session, then paste</span></div><p=
re>Check that my studio is set up correctly. Don't change any files. Test i=
t, don't just read it:
1. Is the marketing plugin turned on for this project only? Compare .claude=
/settings.json with what `claude plugin list` says about its scope.
2. Can you use the plugin here right now? List the plugin commands you can =
see whose names include campaign or brand.
3. If a classmate clones this repo, will they get the same plugin? Explain =
why, using what is actually in the repo.
4. Is .claude/settings.local.json ignored by git? Prove it with `git check-=
ignore -v .claude/settings.local.json`.
Give me a short table: Check | OK or Problem | What you saw.
Then ask me two quick questions, one at a time, about the difference betwee=
n files the whole team shares and files that are only mine.</pre></div>
  <div class=3D"outcomes"><div class=3D"ocard win"><div class=3D"okick">You=
'll know it worked when</div><ul class=3D"win-list">
    <li><code>claude plugin list</code> shows marketing with Scope: project=
.</li>
    <li>You can see <code>/marketing:campaign-plan</code> and <code>/market=
ing:brand-review</code>. The plugin is built for email, so in Step 6 a rule=
 file will make Claude write SMS instead.</li>
    <li><code>git check-ignore</code> names your .gitignore line.</li>
  </ul></div><div class=3D"ocard exam"><div class=3D"okick">Exam note</div>=
<p>Team standards go at project level: CLAUDE.md in the repo, <code>.claude=
/settings.json</code>. Teammates get them when they clone. Your personal fi=
les (<code>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code>, <code>settings.local.json</code>) ne=
ver reach anyone else. If a new teammate isn't getting the instructions, th=
ey sit at user level.</p></div></div>
</section>
<section class=3D"step" id=3D"step-3" data-step=3D"3">
  <div class=3D"step-head"><div class=3D"num">3</div><div class=3D"meta"><d=
iv class=3D"cap"><span class=3D"cap-text">Skill areas: @import, interview p=
attern</span><span class=3D"xchip">3.1</span><span class=3D"xchip">3.5</spa=
n></div><h3>Your brand voice</h3><p class=3D"sub">Let Claude interview you,=
 then keep the voice in its own file that CLAUDE.md imports.</p></div><labe=
l class=3D"done-box"><input type=3D"checkbox" id=3D"done-3"> Done</label></=
div>
  <div class=3D"plain"><p><strong>In plain words:</strong> a tailor takes y=
our measurements before cutting the cloth. Here Claude asks first, then wri=
tes. The <code>@brand/voice.md</code> line is a note pinned to the notice b=
oard that says "also read the brand book".</p></div>
  <div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-head"><span class=3D"chip build">B=
UILD</span><span class=3D"code-desc">Paste in Claude Code</span></div><pre>=
Interview me about my brand so you can write our brand voice guide. Ask one=
 question at a time and wait for my answer. Ask at most six questions.
If I don't have a brand, I'll say "use the sample": Chirpnet Mobile, a smal=
l made-up mobile network launching a cheap late-night data bundle called Th=
e Night Owl Bundle (midnight to 7 am), for prepaid subscribers who opted in=
 to offers. Even with the sample, still ask me every question. I'll make up=
 the answers, because practising the interview is the point.
When you have enough, write brand/voice.md with five parts: our voice in th=
ree words, words we use, words we avoid, claims we must never make (for exa=
mple "unlimited" without a fair-use note, "free" when there are conditions,=
 "fastest network"), and how every message starts and ends (our sender tag =
and opt-out line). Then add the line @brand/voice.md to CLAUDE.md so it loa=
ds in every session.</pre></div>
  <p><strong>Then:</strong> Answer each question. Making things up is fine =
with the sample. When the file is written, ask Claude to commit it.</p>
  <div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-head"><span class=3D"chip check">C=
HECK</span><span class=3D"code-desc">New session, then paste</span></div><p=
re>Test my brand voice setup. Don't change any files.
1. Without opening any file first, write one opening line for the Night Owl=
 Bundle launch text. Then tell me which brand rules you followed and which =
file they came from. (This shows whether brand/voice.md loads by itself.)
2. Now write a line that says the Night Owl Bundle gives truly unlimited fr=
ee data all night. If our rules forbid that, say which rule, and give me a =
safe version instead.
3. Run git status and tell me whether the brand files are committed.
Give me a short table: Test | Pass or Fail | What happened.
Then ask me two quick questions, one at a time, about why the brand rules l=
ive in their own file that CLAUDE.md imports.</pre></div>
  <div class=3D"outcomes"><div class=3D"ocard win"><div class=3D"okick">You=
'll know it worked when</div><ul class=3D"win-list">
    <li>The fresh session follows your voice without opening any file, and =
names <code>brand/voice.md</code> as the source.</li>
    <li>It refuses the "unlimited free" line, names the rule, and offers a =
safe line.</li>
  </ul></div><div class=3D"ocard exam"><div class=3D"okick">Exam note</div>=
<p><code>@import</code> keeps CLAUDE.md small: each project imports only th=
e standards it needs. The interview pattern is for unfamiliar work: Claude =
asks before it builds and raises things you didn't think of.</p><p><strong>=
Exam answer:</strong> to see which memory files are loaded, use <code>/memo=
ry</code>. In today's product, <code>/context</code> shows it.</p></div></d=
iv>
</section>
<section class=3D"step" id=3D"step-4" data-step=3D"4">
  <div class=3D"step-head"><div class=3D"num">4</div><div class=3D"meta"><d=
iv class=3D"cap"><span class=3D"cap-text">Skill area: using a ready-made sk=
ill</span><span class=3D"xchip">3.2</span></div><h3>Plan the campaign</h3><=
p class=3D"sub">Use a ready-made skill from the plugin to turn an idea into=
 a full plan.</p></div><label class=3D"done-box"><input type=3D"checkbox" i=
d=3D"done-4"> Done</label></div>
  <div class=3D"plain"><p><strong>In plain words:</strong> <code>/marketing=
:campaign-plan</code> is a planning form a marketing team already tested. Y=
ou fill in the blanks and it builds the structure.</p></div>
  <div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-head"><span class=3D"chip build">B=
UILD</span><span class=3D"code-desc">Paste in Claude Code</span></div><pre>=
/marketing:campaign-plan Launch The Night Owl Bundle to our opted-in subscr=
ibers.
Goal: 500 subscribers activate the Night Owl Bundle in the first month.
Audience: about 20,000 prepaid subscribers who opted in to offers, in three=
 groups: students, gamers, and budget-callers.
Timeline: 4 weeks, starting the first Monday of next month.
Channel: SMS only, no paid ads. Every message must be sent between 9 am and=
