div class=3D"cap"><span class=3D"cap-text">Skill area: hooks</span><span cl=
ass=3D"xchip">1.5</span></div><h3>The safety guard</h3><p class=3D"sub">Mak=
e a rule Claude cannot break, using a hook.</p></div><label class=3D"done-b=
ox"><input type=3D"checkbox" id=3D"done-10"> Done</label></div>
  <div class=3D"plain"><p><strong>In plain words:</strong> CLAUDE.md asks C=
laude to behave. A hook is a guard at the exchange who checks every text be=
fore it leaves. Claude can't talk its way past the guard.</p></div>
  <div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-head"><span class=3D"chip build">B=
UILD</span><span class=3D"code-desc">Replace [your phone] before you press =
Enter</span></div><pre>Add a safety guard so Claude can never text the wron=
g people, even by mistake. CLAUDE.md asks nicely. This guard makes sure.
1. Write .claude/hooks/send-guard.mjs, a small Node script for a PreToolUse=
 hook. It reads the tool call as JSON from standard input and looks at tool=
_name and tool_input. Tool names arrive like mcp__sms__send-sms, so look at=
 the part after mcp__sms__.
- For send-sms: the "to" number must be +15005550006 or match my own number=
, which comes from the MY_PHONE environment variable. Compare digits only, =
ignoring spaces, dashes and brackets. If it doesn't match, print a short re=
ason that names the number and exit with code 2 to block the call.
- For send-bulk-sms: always block, with a short reason, because it can reac=
h whole lists of people.
- For anything else: exit 0 to allow it.
2. Register it in .claude/settings.json as a PreToolUse hook with the match=
er mcp__sms__.* and this command:
node "$CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR/.claude/hooks/send-guard.mjs"
Keep everything that is already in that file.
3. Put my own number in .claude/settings.local.json like this: "env": { "MY=
_PHONE": "[your phone]" }. That file is only mine and is not committed.
4. Commit the hook and .claude/settings.json.</pre></div>
  <div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-head"><span class=3D"chip check">C=
HECK</span><span class=3D"code-desc">New session, then paste</span></div><p=
re>Test the send guard for real. Don't change any files.
1. Use the sms send-sms tool to send message 01 from campaigns/night-owl-la=
unch/messages/ to Ayesha, the first person in subscribers/subscribers.csv, =
at her number. This should be blocked. Quote the block message word for wor=
d.
2. Send the same message to +15005550006. This should go through.
3. Send it to my own number, the MY_PHONE value in .claude/settings.local.j=
son. This should go through too.
4. Try send-bulk-sms to +15005550006 alone. This should be blocked.
5. Run git check-ignore -v .claude/settings.local.json to prove my number i=
s not committed.
Don't try to get around a block. Give me a short table: Test | Pass or Fail=
 | What happened.
Then ask me two quick questions, one at a time: what the difference is betw=
een the CLAUDE.md rule and this guard, and what exit code 2 means for a hoo=
k.</pre></div>
  <div class=3D"outcomes"><div class=3D"ocard win"><div class=3D"okick">You=
'll know it worked when</div><ul class=3D"win-list">
    <li>The send to Ayesha is blocked, with the guard's own message naming =
her number.</li>
    <li>The sends to <code>+15005550006</code> and to your number go throug=
h. The bulk send is blocked even to the test number.</li>
  </ul></div><div class=3D"ocard exam"><div class=3D"okick">Exam note</div>=
<p><strong>Tip:</strong> Write your number in international format, for exa=
mple <code>+923001234567</code>. The practice server never texts it; it onl=
y becomes the one real number the guard allows. If you ever send for real, =
a Twilio trial account can only text numbers you have verified, so use the =
same one.</p></div></div>
  <div class=3D"note"><p><strong>Exam note:</strong> The guide names only P=
ostToolUse, for cleaning up tool results. It describes blocking as "tool ca=
ll interception" without naming the hook. In the product, <strong>PreToolUs=
e</strong> is what blocks, with exit code 2 or a deny decision, and PostToo=
lUse can't block. On the exam, match the behaviour described.</p></div>
</section>
