<div class=3D"part" id=3D"part3"><div class=3D"part-kick">Part 3 =C2=B7 Ste=
ps 12=E2=80=9314</div><h2>Work like a team</h2><p class=3D"part-sub">Pick w=
ork up across days, try two ideas side by side, review automatically, and p=
ackage your tools for the next client.</p></div>
<section class=3D"step" id=3D"step-12" data-step=3D"12">
  <div class=3D"step-head"><div class=3D"num">12</div><div class=3D"meta"><=
div class=3D"cap"><span class=3D"cap-text">Skill area: sessions</span><span=
 class=3D"xchip">1.7</span></div><h3>Resume and fork</h3><p class=3D"sub">P=
ick up earlier work by name, and try two ideas from the same starting point=
.</p></div><label class=3D"done-box"><input type=3D"checkbox" id=3D"done-12=
"> Done</label></div>
  <div class=3D"plain"><p><strong>In plain words:</strong> a named session =
is a bookmark. A fork is a fork in the road: two paths from the same spot. =
Forks share the files on your computer, but not the conversation.</p></div>
  <ol class=3D"steps-list">
    <li><strong>Part A, start a named session:</strong> <code>claude -n lau=
nch-angles</code><br>Paste: <em>Read campaigns/night-owl-launch/plan.md and=
 each group's version of message 01. In five short bullets, tell me what ea=
ch subscriber group cares about most. Don't write any files.</em> Then <cod=
e>/exit</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Part B, come back later:</strong> <code>claude --resume lau=
nch-angles</code><br>Paste: <em>Remind me: what did you say the gamers care=
 about most? One sentence.</em> Then <code>/exit</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Part C, first fork, with its own name:</strong> <code>claud=
e --resume launch-angles --fork-session -n angle-a</code><br>Paste: <em>Ang=
le A: write three opening lines for message 01 (the words right after "Chir=
pnet:") that focus on saving money at night. Each 50 characters or fewer. S=
ave them to notes/angle-a.md.</em> Then <code>/exit</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Part D, second fork, from the same spot:</strong> <code>cla=
ude --resume launch-angles --fork-session -n angle-b</code><br>Paste: <em>B=
efore you start: do you know anything about an Angle A from our conversatio=
n? Answer yes or no first.</em> Then: <em>Angle B: write three opening line=
s for message 01 that focus on gaming and streaming all night. Each 50 char=
acters or fewer. Save them to notes/angle-b.md.</em> Then <code>/exit</code=
>.</li>
    <li><strong>Part E, start fresh from a summary.</strong> Run <code>clau=
de --resume launch-angles</code> and ask: <em>"Read notes/angle-a.md and no=
tes/angle-b.md. Then write notes/handoff.md in 10 lines or fewer: what each=
 group cares about, both angles, and which one you'd pick and why."</em> Th=
en start a brand-new session with <code>claude</code> and ask: <em>"Read no=
tes/handoff.md and continue from it: write the final version of message 01,=
 following .claude/rules/sms.md."</em></li>
  </ol>
  <div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-head"><span class=3D"chip check">C=
HECK</span><span class=3D"code-desc">New session, then paste</span></div><p=
re>Check my session experiment. Don't change any files.
1. Do notes/angle-a.md and notes/angle-b.md each have three opening lines o=
f 50 characters or fewer? Count them.
2. Does either file use the other angle's idea? They were written in separa=
te forks, so they shouldn't.
3. Is notes/handoff.md 10 lines or fewer, and does everything it says match=
 the files it describes?
Give me a short table: Test | Pass or Fail | What you saw.
Then ask me two quick questions, one at a time: when I should resume a sess=
ion and when I should start fresh with a summary, and what --fork-session g=
ave me that resuming didn't.</pre></div>
  <div class=3D"outcomes"><div class=3D"ocard win"><div class=3D"okick">You=
'll know it worked when</div><ul class=3D"win-list">
    <li>The resumed session remembers what it said about gamers.</li>
    <li>Fork B answers "no" about Angle A's conversation. It may notice the=
 <code>angle-a.md</code> file, because forks share files.</li>
  </ul></div><div class=3D"ocard exam"><div class=3D"okick">Exam note</div>=
<p>To continue a specific earlier investigation, use <code>--resume &lt;ses=
sion-name&gt;</code>. To compare two approaches from one shared starting po=
int, use fork_session; on the command line that's <code>--fork-session</cod=
e>. If the old tool results are stale, start fresh with a structured summar=
y. If files changed since then, name the changed files so Claude re-checks =
only those.</p></div></div>
  <p><strong>If something goes wrong:</strong> if you see "matches 2 sessio=
ns", you forked without <code>-n</code>, so the fork kept the original name=
. Use one of the session ids the error prints, and name forks from now on.<=
/p>
