<section class=3D"step" id=3D"step-13" data-step=3D"13">
  <div class=3D"step-head"><div class=3D"num">13</div><div class=3D"meta"><=
div class=3D"cap"><span class=3D"cap-text">Skill area: headless mode</span>=
<span class=3D"xchip">3.6</span></div><h3>Automatic review</h3><p class=3D"=
sub">Run Claude without chatting, and get results a program can read.</p></=
div><label class=3D"done-box"><input type=3D"checkbox" id=3D"done-13"> Done=
</label></div>
  <div class=3D"plain"><p><strong>In plain words:</strong> instead of chatt=
ing, you hand Claude a job sheet and it hands back a filled-in form. The fo=
rm's shape, called a schema, never changes, so a program can read it.</p></=
div>
  <div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-head"><span class=3D"chip build">B=
UILD</span><span class=3D"code-desc">1 of 2 =C2=B7 Paste in Claude Code</sp=
an></div><pre>Make a review that runs without anyone typing, so it can late=
r run on every pull request.
1. Create review/findings.schema.json: a JSON Schema for an object with a "=
findings" array. Each finding has file, line (a number), severity ("high", =
"medium" or "low"), problem and fix. All five are required.
2. Create scripts/review.mjs, a small Node script that takes a campaign fol=
der and runs Claude Code headless, like this:
claude -p "Review every message in &lt;folder&gt; against brand/voice.md an=
d .claude/rules/sms.md. Measure lengths with a command. Report only real pr=
oblems." --output-format json --json-schema "&lt;the schema file's text&gt;=
" --allowedTools "Read,Glob,Grep,Bash"
The output may be one JSON object, or a list whose last item is the result,=
 so handle both. Take structured_output, save it to review/findings.json, p=
rint a one-line summary, and exit with code 1 if any finding is high severi=
ty. It must work in Windows PowerShell, so call claude through Node's child=
_process without relying on Bash quoting.
3. To prove it catches problems, write a deliberately bad message at campai=
gns/test-bad/messages/01-bad.md. It should break our rules with "truly unli=
mited free data", a "last chance" line, two links, no STOP line, one emoji,=
 and a body over 160 characters. Then run: node scripts/review.mjs campaign=
s/test-bad
4. Run it on campaigns/night-owl-launch too, then commit everything.
Tell me what each run found and what exit code it gave.</pre></div>
  <div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-head"><span class=3D"chip build">B=
UILD</span><span class=3D"code-desc">2 of 2 =C2=B7 Same session</span></div=
><pre>Improve scripts/review.mjs so a second review doesn't repeat itself.
If review/findings.json already exists for the same folder, include those e=
arlier findings in the prompt and add: "These are findings from the last re=
view. Report only problems that are new or still not fixed. Don't repeat on=
es that have been fixed." To make that work, save the folder name inside re=
view/findings.json too.
Then test it:
1. Run node scripts/review.mjs campaigns/test-bad and note the findings.
2. Fix only the "truly unlimited free data" claim in campaigns/test-bad/mes=
sages/01-bad.md (change it to something allowed).
3. Run the review again on campaigns/test-bad.
Tell me: did the unlimited-free finding disappear, and did anything get rep=
orted twice? Commit when done.</pre></div>
  <div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-head"><span class=3D"chip check">C=
HECK</span><span class=3D"code-desc">New session, then paste</span></div><p=
re>Test my automatic review. The script rewrites review/findings.json; don'=
t change any other file.
1. Run node scripts/review.mjs campaigns/test-bad. Did it exit with code 1?=
 Does every finding in review/findings.json have all five fields from revie=
w/findings.schema.json, with severity high, medium or low?
2. Did it catch the missing "Reply STOP to opt out" line and the second lin=
k? Quote those findings.
3. Run node scripts/review.mjs campaigns/night-owl-launch. Did it exit with=
 code 0?
4. Read scripts/review.mjs. Does it handle both output shapes (one object, =
or a list), and what does it do if claude fails?
Give me a short table: Test | Pass or Fail | What you saw.
Then ask me two quick questions, one at a time: why a separate headless run=
 does the review instead of the session that wrote the messages, and why we=
 ask for JSON that matches a schema instead of plain text.</pre></div>
  <div class=3D"outcomes"><div class=3D"ocard win"><div class=3D"okick">You=
'll know it worked when</div><ul class=3D"win-list">
    <li>The bad message gives exit code 1 with findings like the unlimited-=
free claim, the missing STOP line and the second link. Your real campaign g=
ives exit code 0.</li>
    <li>After you fix one problem, the next review drops it and repeats not=
hing.</li>
    <li>Reviews vary a little from run to run. That's why the script only f=
ails on high severity.</li>
  </ul></div><div class=3D"ocard exam"><div class=3D"okick">Exam note</div>=
<p><code>-p</code> runs Claude without waiting for input, which fixes a CI =
job that hangs. <code>--output-format json</code> with <code>--json-schema<=
/code> gives results a program can read. CLAUDE.md holds the review rules f=
or CI runs. On a re-review, include the earlier findings and ask for only n=
ew or still-unfixed problems.</p><p><strong>Not real:</strong> <code>CLAUDE=
_HEADLESS=3Dtrue</code> and <code>--batch</code>. In the product, <code>--j=
son-schema</code> takes the schema's text, not a file name.</p></div></div>
  <p><strong>Optional: every pull request.</strong> If your studio is on Gi=
tHub, ask Claude to add a GitHub Actions workflow that runs <code>node scri=
pts/review.mjs</code> on pull requests. It needs your <code>ANTHROPIC_API_K=
EY</code> saved as a repository secret.</p>
</section>
<section class=3D"step" id=3D"step-14" data-step=3D"14">
  <div class=3D"step-head"><div class=3D"num">14</div><div class=3D"meta"><=
div class=3D"cap"><span class=3D"cap-text">Skill area: plugins, plan mode</=
span><span class=3D"xchip">3.4</span></div><h3>Package your studio kit</h3>=
