<h2 id=3D"words" class=3D"plain-h2">Words you'll see</h2>
<ul class=3D"plainlist">
  <li><strong>Session:</strong> one conversation with Claude Code. <code>/e=
xit</code> ends it; running <code>claude</code> starts a new one.</li>
  <li><strong>Commit:</strong> a saved snapshot of your files in git. You c=
an always go back to it.</li>
  <li><strong>Plugin:</strong> a package of skills, commands, hooks and con=
nections that you install in one go.</li>
  <li><strong>Skill:</strong> saved instructions Claude follows when you ca=
ll it with <code>/name</code>, or when the task fits.</li>
  <li><strong>MCP server:</strong> a connection that gives Claude tools fro=
m another service, like Twilio.</li>
  <li><strong>Hook:</strong> a small script Claude Code runs at a set momen=
t, like just before a tool runs. It can block the action.</li>
  <li><strong>Practice server:</strong> the Twilio emulator (<code>npx emul=
ate@0.12.3 start --service twilio</code>), a pretend Twilio on your own lap=
top at <code>127.0.0.1:4000</code>.</li>
  <li><strong>Segment:</strong> one billed piece of a text message. Up to 1=
60 plain characters fit in one; longer texts are split and charged per segm=
ent.</li>
  <li><strong>Plain characters (GSM-7):</strong> the basic letters, digits =
and symbols every phone handles. An emoji, a curly quote or Urdu script swi=
tches the whole message to Unicode, where only 70 characters fit in a segme=
nt.</li>
  <li><strong>Sender tag:</strong> the brand name at the start of a message=
, like <code>Chirpnet:</code>, so people know who is texting them.</li>
  <li><strong>Opt-out line:</strong> "Reply STOP to opt out". It lets peopl=
e stop your messages, and many countries require it.</li>
  <li><strong>Test number:</strong> <code>+15005550006</code>, a number Twi=
lio keeps for testing. In this studio it plays the part of <code>delivered@=
resend.dev</code>.</li>
  <li><strong>Fresh session:</strong> a new conversation that doesn't remem=
ber the old one, which makes it a fair checker.</li>
</ul>


